      "final_text": "我们的看法：美加贸易谈判上周破裂，美国随即依据第338条款对约200亿的加拿大出口商品征收50%关税（旨在应对针对美国企业的歧视性做法），涉及金额约占加拿大对美出口总额的5.5%。相较于美国每年约3.5万亿的商品进口总额，该规模微乎其微，因此目前对美国通胀和经济增长的冲击可能微不足道。然而，有两条潜在的升级路径可能改变这一局面。其一，美国汽车关税构成风险——特朗普威胁将于明年1月起对加拿大汽车及相关零部件征收50%关税。汽车贸易流量远大于当前受影响商品，且两国产业高度融合，一旦付诸实施，将对边境两侧的供应链和价格产生实质性影响。其二，加拿大可能通过石油和能源实施报复。加拿大是美国原油进口的最大来源国，2025年日均供应约3.9百万桶，约占美国原油进口总量（约6.2百万桶/日）的63%。加拿大若采取任何限制或切断原油出口的举措，都将造成重大冲击——届时一场局部贸易纷争可能演变为影响美国能源价格的宏观事件。美国能源形势已因伊朗冲突而承压，库存持续消耗，炼油产能利用率亦已接近上限。总体而言，现行关税措施伤及加拿大地区制造业，而非美国经济。但汽车关税与加拿大石油是两个可能迅速打破这相对温和的局面的因素。",
      "iterations": 2,
      "seg_status": "edited",
      "source_text": "What we think US-Canada trade talks fell through last week and the US followed through on 50% Section 338 tariffs (meant to address discriminatory practices against US companies) covering roughly $20bn of Canadian exports, covering about 5.5% of Canada's exports to the US. That amount is minimal relative to ~$3.5T in annual US goods imports, so the hit to US inflation and growth is likely to be negligible for now. However, there are two potential paths of escalation which can move the needle. First, US auto tariffs is a risk with Trump's threatened 50% tariff on Canadian autos and related components set to begin in January. Autos are a materially larger and more integrated trade flow than the currently impacted goods, and a move here would have real supply-chain and price consequences on both sides of the border. The second risk is Canadian retaliation via oil and energy. Canada is the largest source of US crude oil imports, supplying ~3.9 million barrels per day in 2025 — roughly 63% of total US crude imports (of ~6.2 million barrels per day). Any Canadian move to curb or cut off crude exports would be a shock, and this is where a contained trade spat could turn into a macro event for US energy prices. The US energy situation is already stressed from the conflict in Iran, which has driven inventory depletion and maxed out refinery utilization. In all, the tariffs as they stand hurt Canadian regional manufacturing and not the US economy. But auto tariffs and Canadian oil are two channels that could change this benign picture quickly.",
